" System vimrc for Vim on ESP32-P4 / M5Stack Tab5.
"
" Vim sources this ($VIM/vimrc, and $VIM is /vimrt) before the user's own vimrc
" and, when there is none, before defaults.vim. It sets what is different about
" THIS platform; editing taste is left to defaults.vim and the user.
"
" Put personal settings in /fat/.vimrc -- /vimrt is a read-only partition.

" No locale support on the device, so Vim would otherwise default to latin1.
set encoding=utf-8
scriptencoding utf-8

" The medium is wear-levelled SPI flash. Swap files, backups, undo files and
" viminfo would all be written continuously, and FATFS reports st_ino = 0, so
" Vim's same-file detection for backups is blind anyway (docs/PHASE1.md).
set noswapfile
set nobackup nowritebackup
set noundofile
set viminfo=

" Files arrive over SCP, SFTP, USB and the web interface. Modelines let a file
" set options when it is opened, which is not something to trust by default.
set nomodeline

" The backtracking regexp engine. Measured on stock desktop Vim built from the
" same source, the first file opened costs 26.5 MB under the default automatic
" engine (compiling filetype.vim's ~1600 autocmd patterns as NFA programs) and
" 0.6 MB under this one. On the P4 the difference is about 19 MB of the 32 MB of
" PSRAM. It does not recurse on the C stack -- regexp_bt.c keeps its state on a
" heap "regstack" capped by 'maxmempattern' -- and pathological patterns are cut
" off by the regexp timeout, which the port implements (esp_shims.c, setitimer).
set regexpengine=1

" Colour. The builtin xterm termcap (term.c, builtin_xterm) has no colour
" entries at all: an ordinary build gets them from terminfo, which this one does
" not have (HAVE_TGETENT is off). Without this, t_Co is 0, defaults.vim never
" turns syntax on, and highlighting would be monochrome regardless.
"
" Both terminals Vim talks to here do 256 colours: a host terminal over the
" UART, and the Tab5's libvterm-based display console. Vim's term_color()
" upgrades the plain "\e[3%dm" forms to 38;5;N / 48;5;N itself above 15.
if &term =~# '^xterm'
  set t_Co=256
  let &t_AB = "\<Esc>[4%dm"
  let &t_AF = "\<Esc>[3%dm"
endif

" Mouse: SGR reporting. It is the only xterm mouse protocol that works beyond
" column 223 (the Tab5 console is 160 columns at 8 px, wider in future), and
" both terminals used here -- host terminals and libvterm -- support it.
" Pinned rather than detected: detection needs a terminal version response,
" which a scripted or piped session never sends.
set ttymouse=sgr
